WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.180
Consider a workflow that receives twelve customer records.

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:11.480
For each customer, the workflow calls an external enrichment API, normalizes the response, and prepares a database update.

3
00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:16.860
The API is sensitive to request pacing, so the initial design should send one customer at a time.

4
00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:22.720
This is a good candidate for Loop Over Items because the repeated work is not a single isolated node.

5
00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:26.980
It is a controlled body made of several steps that must finish in order.

6
00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:31.180
The conceptual workflow begins with a trigger or data-source node.

7
00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:33.760
That node produces the customer records.

8
00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:37.940
The records move into Loop Over Items with Batch Size set to one.

9
00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:45.400
The loop output then sends the current customer to an HTTP Request node or to a simulated enrichment step in a training environment.

10
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:52.300
After enrichment, an Edit Fields or Code node normalizes the response into a predictable shape.

11
00:00:52.400 --> 00:01:00.400
A database update or simulated destination writes the result, and that last required operation connects back to Loop Over Items.

12
00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:04.440
Expressions inside the loop body should refer to the current customer item.

13
00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:15.500
Fields such as customerId, email, account tier, or region need to remain available long enough for the request, the normalization step, and the destination update.

14
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:22.560
A stable customer identifier is especially important because an external response may not preserve the same shape as the input.

15
00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:31.780
If the original identifier disappears halfway through the body, a later node may know that it has enrichment data but no longer know which customer should receive it.

16
00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:36.860
The return connection should originate after the last operation that must complete for the current iteration.

17
00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:42.240
In this scenario, that means after the database update or the simulated destination step.

18
00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:51.060
If the workflow returns to Loop Over Items immediately after the HTTP Request, the normalization and update might no longer be part of the controlled loop body.

19
00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:56.040
The canvas would still contain those nodes, but the sequence would no longer match the design intent.

20
00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.960
The completion action belongs on the done path.

21
00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:05.780
A completion email, audit record, or final summary inside the loop body would run once for each customer.

22
00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:09.240
With twelve customers, that means twelve completion messages.

23
00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:21.100
The done path represents work after iteration has finished, so it is the correct place to send a single completion notice or calculate a summary of processed, skipped, failed, and retried records.

24
00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:26.260
Increasing Batch Size from one to three changes the release pattern.

25
00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:30.040
Each iteration now sends three customer items into the body.

26
00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:35.900
That can be useful when the API and the downstream nodes can handle small groups safely.

27
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:43.360
It does not automatically guarantee true parallel network requests, and it does not remove the need to inspect the downstream node behavior.

28
00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:46.820
It simply changes how many items the loop releases per pass.

29
00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:51.260
This scenario is also a good place to practice evidence-based testing.

30
00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:56.640
Run the design first with three synthetic customers that have obvious identifiers.

31
00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:08.180
Confirm that the API step receives one customer per pass, the normalization step keeps the same customerId, and the destination step receives the normalized result for that same customer.

32
00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:13.080
Then inspect the done path and confirm that the completion summary runs one time.

33
00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:19.980
After that small test is clean, increase the sample to twelve records and compare expected and completed counts.

34
00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:22.500
Error handling should follow the same boundary.

35
00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:27.800
A handled per-customer failure belongs inside the body because it describes one customer's result.

36
00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:33.580
A final alert about the whole run belongs after done because it describes the completed execution.

37
00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:40.160
Separating those two responsibilities keeps the workflow readable when some records succeed and others require follow-up.

38
00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:48.520
A controlled loop is reliable when the body has a clear start, a clear end, preserved identity, and a one-time completion path.

39
00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:56.060
Start with a conservative batch size, confirm the execution data with a tiny set of records, and only then adjust the design for throughput.

40
00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:58.720
The goal is not just to make the loop run.

41
00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:02.340
The goal is to make every customer update traceable and repeatable.
